  "title": "The Ionization of Weak Acids and Weak Bases (CyberLearn)",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4wwvkkan79sxmeo",
                "text": "Ionization of Weak Acids"
              }
            ],
            "id": "oblaeca5s9e53sa",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Many acids and bases are weak; that is, they do not ionize fully in aqueous solution. A solution of a weak acid in water is a mixture of the nonionized acid, hydronium ion, and the conjugate base of the acid, with the nonionized acid present in the greatest concentration. Thus, a weak acid increases the hydronium ion concentration in an aqueous solution (but not nearly as much as the same amount of a strong acid)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "g7zk2ojs3nz515g",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Acetic acid, CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "H, is a weak acid. When we add acetic acid to water, it ionizes to a small extent according to the equation:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ezm45bdm7japxin",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "yas2r1d8fayjscu",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tx2ao029dai1mm4",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{CH}}_3{\\mathrm{CO}}_2\\mathrm H(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm{CH}}_3{\\mathrm{CO}}_2^-(aq),",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "giving an equilibrium mixture with most of the acid present in the nonionized (molecular) form. This equilibrium, like other equilibria, is dynamic; acetic acid molecules donate hydrogen ions to water molecules and form hydronium ions and acetate ions at the same rate that hydronium ions donate hydrogen ions to acetate ions to reform acetic acid molecules and water molecules. We can tell by measuring the pH of an aqueous solution of known concentration that only a fraction of the weak acid is ionized at any moment. The remaining weak acid is present in the nonionized form."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1l10ivch1053yjv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For acetic acid, at equilibrium:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "o1tad5dm7aufl8b",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "9c0g8gtmjxd2ff1",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0cx69lfwy78oter",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_a\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack\\lbrack{\\mathrm{CH}}_3{\\mathrm{CO}}_2^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm{CH}}_3{\\mathrm{CO}}_2\\mathrm H\\rbrack}\\;=\\;1.8\\times10^{-5}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          }
