          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "6nuc5o88g0jd084",
                "text": "Ionization of Weak Bases"
              }
            ],
            "id": "bvbwezkfpne590f",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "At equilibrium, a solution of a weak base in water is a mixture of the nonionized base, the conjugate acid of the weak base, and hydroxide ion with the nonionized base present in the greatest concentration. Thus, a weak base increases the hydroxide ion concentration in an aqueous solution (but not as much as the same amount of a strong base)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "q8nr3s39dawewgh",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For example, a solution of the weak base trimethylamine, (CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "N, in water reacts according to the equation:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dl59ksir8ghzdva",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "i8xxkqtg3vky3rt",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "jhz9rpmr7glxvrc",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "({\\mathrm{CH}}_3)_3\\mathrm N(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;({\\mathrm{CH}}_3)_3\\mathrm{NH}^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq),",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "giving an equilibrium mixture with most of the base present as the nonionized amine. This equilibrium is analogous to that described for weak acids."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2z5xe5cv67o6qd5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We can confirm by measuring the pH of an aqueous solution of a weak base of known concentration that only a fraction of the base reacts with water. The remaining weak base is present as the unreacted form. The equilibrium constant for the ionization of a weak base, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "sub": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is called the ionization constant of the weak base, and is equal to the reaction quotient when the reaction is at equilibrium. For trimethylamine, at equilibrium: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "pek3p8lc73wrc99",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "q6xss41zhutx4n5",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tsf3ahbvh3hvbcx",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_b\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack({\\mathrm{CH}}_3)_3\\mathrm{NH}^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{OH}^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack({\\mathrm{CH}}_3)_3\\mathrm N\\rbrack}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          }
        ],
